r\n> github.com/microsoft:\r\n>   - user: msemployee\r\n>     oauth_token: ...\r\n> \r\n> github.com:\r\n>   - user: personal\r\n>     oauth_token: ...\r\n> ```\r\n> \r\n> **How will it benefit CLI and its users?**\r\n> \r\n> It will allow Github users with multiple accounts of varying access to seamlessly work on different repositories.\r\n\r\n","isMinimized":true,"minimizedReason":"SPAM"},{"author":{"__typename":"User","login":"gggjfjfk"},"createdAt":"2024-09-20T16:25:24Z","lastEditedAt":null,"body":"[](url)","isMinimized":true,"minimizedReason":"SPAM"},{"author":{"__typename":"User","login":"kianpittong051199"},"createdAt":"2024-11-23T15:24:02Z","lastEditedAt":null,"body":"refund\r\n","isMinimized":true,"minimizedReason":"SPAM"},{"author":{"__typename":"User","login":"Jadiu1920"},"createdAt":"2024-11-30T11:34:55Z","lastEditedAt":null,"body":"Dobrze jest ","isMinimized":true,"minimizedReason":"SPAM"},{"author":{"__typename":"User","login":"Jasonradford51"},"createdAt":"2025-01-27T18:12:46Z","lastEditedAt":null,"body":"H","isMinimized":true,"minimizedReason":"SPAM"},{"author":{"__typename":"User","login":"Jasonradford51"},"createdAt":"2025-01-27T18:12:58Z","lastEditedAt":null,"body":"Completely ","isMinimized":true,"minimizedReason":"SPAM"},{"author":{"__typename":"User","login":"bmitc"},"createdAt":"2025-05-11T21:57:39Z","lastEditedAt":null,"body":"I believe this is supported now with `gh auth switch`:\n\n```console\n> gh --version\ngh version 2.67.0 (2025-02-11)\nhttps://github.com/cli/cli/releases/tag/v2.67.0\n\n> gh auth switch --help\nSwitch the active account for a GitHub host.\n\nThis command changes the authentication configuration that will\nbe used when running commands targeting the specified GitHub host.\n\nIf the specified host has two accounts, the active account will be switched\nautomatically. If there are more than two accounts, disambiguation will be\nrequired either through the `--user` flag or an interactive prompt.\n\nFor a list of authenticated accounts you can run `gh auth status`.\n\n\nUSAGE\n  gh auth switch [flags]\n\nFLAGS\n  -h, --hostname string   The hostname of the GitHub instance to switch account for\n  -u, --user string       The account to switch to\n\nINHERITED FLAGS\n  --help   Show help for command\n\nEXAMPLES\n  # Select what host and account to switch to via a prompt\n  $ gh auth switch\n\n  # Switch the active account on a specific host to a specific user\n  $ gh auth switch --hostname enterprise.internal --user monalisa\n\nLEARN MORE\n  Use `gh <command> <subcommand> --help` for more information about a command.\n  Read the manual at https://cli.github.com/manual\n  Learn about exit codes using `gh help exit-codes`\n```\n\nI was able to login to multiple accounts using `gh auth login` and then switch between them with `gh auth switch`.","isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":null},{"author":{"__typename":"User","login":"uncenter"},"createdAt":"2025-07-02T14:12:11Z","lastEditedAt":null,"body":"> I believe this is supported now with `gh auth switch`:\n> I was able to login to multiple accounts using `gh auth login` and then switch between them with `gh auth switch`.\n\nMaybe this is worth a separate issue but since it comes up a lot in this issue - what's currently the best way to *automatically* switch between accounts along the lines of Git's `includeIf`? I'd love to have `gh` use one account or another based on my current `user.email` (set variably by the aforementioned `includeIf`)...","isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":null},{"author":{"__typename":"User","login":"williammartin"},"createdAt":"2025-07-02T14:35:02Z","lastEditedAt":null,"body":"@uncenter we were just talking this week about splitting all the SSH multi-account desires into a new issue since there is so much conversation in this one. Stay tuned!","isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":null},{"author":{"__typename":"User","login":"gotgenes"},"createdAt":"2025-07-02T18:23:18Z","lastEditedAt":"2025-07-02T18:23:42Z","body":"> [@uncenter](https://github.com/uncenter) we were just talking this week about splitting all the SSH multi-account desires into a new issue since there is so much conversation in this one. Stay tuned!\n\n@williammartin I feel confused by your reply. Nothing in @uncenter's comment mentioned SSH. They asked if there is a way to get gh CLI to detect or be told to use a specific account when in a certain path. For example, if my current working directory is under `~/work`, I want gh to use my work account; otherwise, I want to use my personal account.\n\nI don't yet see how this ties to the network protocol (SSH vs HTTP).","isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":null},{"author":{"__typename":"User","login":"bmitc"},"createdAt":"2025-07-02T20:46:57Z","lastEditedAt":null,"body":"Automatically switching would definitely be nice. My use case is that my company uses an EMU managed GitHub Enterprise which uses a separate GitHub account than the public GitHub, even though they both are hosted on github.com. So I keep one directory for the regular github.com and another for my company's specific github.com that's EMU managed. Right now, I have to manually remember to run `gh auth switch` when working between them, but having this be automatic based upon something (an environment variable, `git` configuration, directory regex, etc.) would be very nice.\n\nI suppose writing a script is in order, but perhaps an official solution in the `gh` tool would be nice.","isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":null},{"author":{"__typename":"User","login":"vbrozik"},"createdAt":"2025-07-03T08:23:00Z","lastEditedAt":"2025-07-23T09:14:44Z","body":"## A working procedure for local clones of GitHub repositories\n\nCurrently, I also use a personal and an enterprise GitHub account. The main obstacle for me was performing Git operations with local copies of repositories from both the accounts. Finally I have found a solution that replaces the `gh auth git-credential` authentication manager with the standard but more capable [**Git Credential Manager**](https://github.com/git-ecosystem/git-credential-manager) (**GCM**) in the Git configuration (but not in the gh client).\n\nGCM is distributed together with Git. Depending on a platform, GCM could store your credentials much more safely than gh client. GCM explicitly [supports multiple users](https://github.com/git-ecosystem/git-credential-manager/blob/release/docs/multiple-users.md) without manual switching.\n\n### How to setup GCM?\n\nAdd your GitHub accounts to the GCM. Yes, you can add multiple accounts:\n\n``` shell\ngit credential-manager github login\n```\n\nConfigure the local copy of a repository. Optionally you can configure Git globally to use the GCM so that in individual repositories you will update just the `remote.origin.url`:\n\n``` shell\n# Either set GCM once globally:\ngit config set --global credential.helper \"\"\ngit config set --global --append credential.helper manager\n# In WSL you can use GCM from the Windows installation of Git\n# if you want to avoid managing multiple installations:\ngit config set --global --append credential.helper \"/mnt/c/Program\\ Files\\ \\(x86\\)/Git\\ Credential\\ Manager/git-credential-manager.exe\"\n\n# ... or set the GCM locally just in the current repository:\ngit config set --local credential.helper \"\"\ngit config set --local --append credential.helper manager\n\n# In the repository, show the current remote origin URL:\ngit config get --local remote.origin.url\n# Insert the corresponding username into the URL:\ngit config set --local remote.origin.url https://vbrozik@github.com/vbrozik/vb-shell-tools.git\n#                             This part is added ^^^^^^^^\n\n# Test in the case of a private repository\ngit fetch -v\n```\n\n### Suggestions for `gh` client development\n\nIt would be really great if `gh repo clone` was extended to (optionally) include the username in the `remote.origin.url` so that manual post-processing using `git config set --local remote.origin.url ...` is not needed.\n\nMaybe the `gh` client credential helper should be discouraged and `gh` client should help the user to configure and use the standard GCM instead? Cannot the `gh` client itself use it too?","isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":null},{"author":{"__typename":"User","login":"williammartin"},"createdAt":"2025-07-03T09:27:52Z","lastEditedAt":null,"body":"@gotgenes, sorry, that was an exceptionally poor explanation on my part. 😅 \n\nI meant to convey that splitting this issue already was an active topic of conversation internally (with regards SSH), so it's likely we'll be going through all the comments and trying to break apart all the different feature requests, which would also involve looking at `includeIf`. Thanks for setting me up to clarify for everyone.","isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":null},{"author":{"__typename":"User","login":"MiK35402"},"createdAt":"2025-07-22T21:47:06Z","lastEditedAt":null,"body":"> ### Describe the feature or problem you’d like to solve\n> \n> I have two accounts on github each with access to a different set of private repositories. Currently I use a combination of git's `[IncludeIf ...]` config and a custom config which overrides the SSH private key I use for certain repositories. E.g.:\n> \n> In `~/.gitconfig`:\n> \n> ```\n> [includeIf \"gitdir:~/work/\"]\n>   path = ~/work/.gitconfig\n> ```\n> \n> and in `~/work/.gitconfig`:\n> ```\n> [core]\n>   sshCommand = \"ssh -i ~/.ssh/work\"\n> ```\n> \n> It will be great if the credentials for `gh` can also be configured per repository as well.\n> \n> ### Proposed solution\n> \n> There are a bunch of options I can think of:\n> \n> * Allow overriding `gh` config by a file in a local clone's `.git` directory\n> * Allow defining rules in the `gh` config which defines which repos/orgs should use which credential. E.g.\n> \n> ```\n> github.com/google:\n>   - user: gemployee\n>     oauth_token: ...\n> \n> github.com/microsoft:\n>   - user: msemployee\n>     oauth_token: ...\n> \n> github.com:\n>   - user: personal\n>     oauth_token: ...\n> ```\n> \n> **How will it benefit CLI and its users?**\n> \n> It will allow Github users with multiple accounts of varying access to seamlessly work on different repositories.\n\n","isMinimized":true,"minimizedReason":"SPAM"},{"author":{"__typename":"User","login":"MSch"},"createdAt":"2025-07-26T15:47:27Z","lastEditedAt":null,"body":"FYI I vibecoded a POC that already works for my needs: https://github.com/cli/cli/pull/11388 ","isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":null},{"author":{"__typename":"User","login":"drmercer-lucid"},"createdAt":"2026-01-05T19:35:21Z","lastEditedAt":null,"body":"I've tried a few different workarounds for this, and ended up just writing a little script and putting it on my path as `gh`, overriding the actual `gh` executable. This way, any other scripts/tools that call `gh` will first switch the user appropriately. This script sets the user based on the presence of a string in the current path, but you can customize the logic here however you want.\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n\n# Set the gh user based on the repo being accessed\n\nif [[ \"$PWD\" == *acme* ]]; then\n    # use myusername2 for acme repos\n    /usr/bin/gh auth switch -u myusername2 >/dev/null 2>&1\nelse\n    # otherwise use myusername1\n    /usr/bin/gh auth switch -u myusername1  >/dev/null 2>&1\nfi\n\n/usr/bin/gh \"$@\"\n```","isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":null},{"author":{"__typename":"User","login":"dotnvo"},"createdAt":"2026-02-24T20:53:34Z","lastEditedAt":null,"body":"> I believe this is supported now with `gh auth switch`:\n> \n> > gh --version\n> gh version 2.67.0 (2025-02-11)\n> https://github.com/cli/cli/releases/tag/v2.67.0\n> \n> > gh auth switch --help\n> Switch the active account for a GitHub host.\n> \n> This command changes the authentication configuration that will\n> be used when running commands targeting the specified GitHub host.\n> \n> If the specified host has two accounts, the active account will be switched\n> automatically. If there are more than two accounts, disambiguation will be\n> required either through the `--user` flag or an interactive prompt.\n> \n> For a list of authenticated accounts you can run `gh auth status`.\n> \n> \n> USAGE\n>   gh auth switch [flags]\n> \n> FLAGS\n>   -h, --hostname string   The hostname of the GitHub instance to switch account for\n>   -u, --user string       The account to switch to\n> \n> INHERITED FLAGS\n>   --help   Show help for command\n> \n> EXAMPLES\n>   # Select what host and account to switch to via a prompt\n>   $ gh auth switch\n> \n>   # Switch the active account on a specific host to a specific user\n>   $ gh auth switch --hostname enterprise.internal --user monalisa\n> \n> LEARN MORE\n>   Use `gh <command> <subcommand> --help` for more information about a command.\n>   Read the manual at https://cli.github.com/manual\n>   Learn about exit codes using `gh help exit-codes`\n> I was able to login to multiple accounts using `gh auth login` and then switch between them with `gh auth switch`.\n\nInteresting, though when I do this with a Github Enterprise account, switching to the github enterprise, it tells me its now the active account, but when i run `gh repo list [owner]` it does not pull from my org, and still pulls from github.com.\n\n```powershell\ngh auth status\n# output shows both accounts logged in one from enterprise url and one from github.com\ngh auth switch --hostname github.xxx.xxx --user xxxxx\n#outputs:\n# ✓ Switched active account for github.xxx.xx to xxxx\ngh repo list\n# outputs personal repos on github.com - the usernames are different as well, but i'm also specifiying the host in the switch.\n```\nMaybe something I'm still doing wrong but feels like this might be a potential issue with `gh auth switch`.\n","isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":null}]}}}}}